## A streamlined .gitignore for modern .NET projects
## including temporary files, build results, and
## files generated by popular .NET tools. If you are
## developing with Visual Studio, the VS .gitignore
## https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/VisualStudio.gitignore
## has more thorough IDE-specific entries.
##
## Get latest from https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/Dotnet.gitignore

# Build results
[Dd]ebug/
[Dd]ebugPublic/
[Rr]elease/
[Rr]eleases/
x64/
x86/
[Ww][Ii][Nn]32/
[Aa][Rr][Mm]/
[Aa][Rr][Mm]64/
bld/
[Bb]in/
# ...except the MCP package's launcher, which is a package `bin` in npm's sense and not a
# build output. The .NET rule above is a directory name, and without this line the one
# file an MCP host is pointed at would be the one file git never saw.
!web/mcp/bin/
[Oo]bj/
[Ll]og/
[Ll]ogs/

# .NET Core
project.lock.json
project.fragment.lock.json
artifacts/

# ASP.NET Scaffolding
ScaffoldingReadMe.txt

# NuGet Packages
*.nupkg
# NuGet Symbol Packages
*.snupkg

# dotenv environment variables file
.env

# Others
~$*
*~
CodeCoverage/

# MSBuild Binary and Structured Log
*.binlog

# MSTest test Results
[Tt]est[Rr]esult*/
[Bb]uild[Ll]og.*

# NUnit
*.VisualState.xml
TestResult.xml
nunit-*.xml

## ── Node / Next.js (web/) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/
.next/
out/
.turbo/
*.tsbuildinfo
.pnpm-store/
npm-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

## ── Playwright (tests/e2e) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
test-results/
playwright-report/
blob-report/
.playwright/

## ── Secrets: never committed. secrets.env.example IS committed and documents
## every variable by tier (REPO-BASELINE.md §2, P5). ────────────────────────────
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
!**/testdata/**/*.pem

## ── LaTeX build output. Only the .tex is committed: a checked-in PDF disagrees
## with its source the first time the source changes (INIT §9). ─────────────────
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.toc
docs/papers/*.pdf

## The diagrams the papers include, rendered to vector PDF by scripts/render-diagrams.mjs.
## Same rule, one directory: a .mmd is the single source of a diagram, and GitHub renders it
## from docs/DIAGRAMS.md without any of this existing. These files exist only so a PDF can
## carry the same picture, and they are regenerated by the workflow that builds the PDF. Note
## the per-directory pattern rather than a bare *.pdf: the pattern says which build it belongs
## to, and a bare one would silently swallow a PDF somebody meant to commit elsewhere.
docs/diagrams/rendered/

## ── Local tool state ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.last-image-tag

## A parallel agent run creates one git worktree per agent under this directory, and a
## worktree is a FULL CHECKOUT of this repository — locked, on its own branch, pointing at
## the same .git. Without this entry the first `git add -A` during such a run commits a copy
## of the repository into itself, and `git status` is unreadable for as long as the agents
## are working. Only the worktrees are ignored: .claude/settings.json is committed, because
## it is the shared configuration rather than one machine's scratch.
.claude/worktrees/

## ── Fetched book content. web/content/book.lock.json and web/content/README.md ARE
## committed; the tree they describe is not. ADR-0008 makes the book's lab engine a versioned
## artefact fetched at a pinned revision, and scripts/fetch-book-content.sh verifies a
## sha256 per file as it writes — so the reviewable artefact is the pin, and the files
## are its output. Without this entry the claim "nothing under content/book is authored
## here" is a request rather than a rule: seven untracked files sit in `git status` for
## ever and the first `git add -A` makes them source. ADR-0013 explains why the directory
## sits under web/ rather than at the repository root.
##
## The __pycache__ entry is not hypothetical. A local `python3 web/content/book/lab/check.py`
## leaves four .pyc files in that tree; prepare-lab-assets.mjs copies from the lock's file
## list rather than walking the directory precisely so a stale .pyc can never be served to
## Pyodide, and this keeps them out of a commit as well. ────────────────────────────────
/web/content/book/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

## The compiled content bundle — book.lock.json's `contentBundle`. Same rule, one file
## instead of eight: it is compiled by scripts/fetch-book-content.sh from the book's own
## programs/{en,pl} at the pinned revision, and nothing under it is authored here either.
/web/content/bundle/

## ── Visual Studio IDE state. .vs/ is one developer's machine: the solution's .suo,
## the window layout, the design-time build cache and the Copilot semantic index —
## binary, rewritten on every keystroke, and meaningless in anyone else's checkout.
## The repository's shared IDE configuration lives in .editorconfig and
## .claude/settings.json, which ARE committed. ──────────────────────────────────
.vs/

## ── launchSettings.json, with exactly one exception. Visual Studio writes one on first F5
## carrying that machine's randomly-assigned localhost ports; for a SERVICE that file is
## pure scratch, because the AppHost is the composition root (P1) and Aspire assigns every
## service's address — a committed profile would be a second, disagreeing answer to "where
## does this run".
##
## The AppHost's own profile is the exception, and it is not a preference. `dotnet run`
## without a profile runs in the PRODUCTION environment, and .NET loads user secrets only in
## Development — so AddParameter("auth-signing-key", secret: true) finds nothing, both
## parameters resolve to ValueMissing, and postgres, pgadmin and authservice are never
## created. The symptom is the worst kind: the dashboard comes up, the log reports no error,
## and the container list is simply empty. README §"Quick start" promises that one command
## works from a fresh clone, so the file that makes it true is committed. ───────────────
**/Properties/launchSettings.json
!src/AbOvo.AppHost/Properties/launchSettings.json

## ── Next.js writes these into web/app/ from its own postinstall, so they reappear on every
## `pnpm install` and are derived rather than authored. The AGENTS.md that IS committed is
## the one at the repository root. Ignoring them keeps `git status` empty after an install,
## which is the only state in which an unexpected file means something. ──────────────────
/web/app/AGENTS.md
/web/app/CLAUDE.md
